A||||8||FieroSTETZ||Legality of V-8 conversion||||6
Z||000000||FieroSTETZ||09-14-1999||01:20 AM||||I am a 17 year old speed junkie! I have had so much trouble with my Fiero 2M6 that I have been considering a V-8 conversion kit, but i have a few questions...<P>1) what is the absolute cheapest i can get away with? (4spd man...)<BR>2) How difficult is the installation? (to gague my skill, i have installed/removed my fiero's engine 2x)<BR>3) Smog legality... i live in CA, is it gonna pass?<BR>4) add'tl equipment needed (exhaust, etc...)<BR>5) does it affect the handling characteristics at all?<BR>and 6) i have a 1989 350 smallblock set aside, what kind of fuel economy can i expect w/ either a 2bbl carb or a 4bbl carb?||||reg||6
Z||000001||theogre||09-14-1999||08:53 AM||||If you use a CARB aproved motor, with CARB aproved addon parts, and it's running properly, then it should pass the smog test.<P>This conversion is extreamly difficult. It requires rewiring much of the back half of the car. It requires numerous custom parts be purchased or fabricated.<P>Yes it will effect handling. Allot. No it won't be a positive change under allot of conditions unless you also rework the whole suspension. Aside from being heavier, the added power realy screws around with the alignment of the whole back of the car.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-14-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||09-14-1999||01:58 PM||||Getting a BAR sticker in California would be the least of the worries. Of the people I talked to a pressed on the subject, most admit they wouldn't do a V8 swap again (maybe a NS, that's horribly pricey).<P>Consider a Buick V6, DOHC, or a 3.4L upgrade. Cleaner and more "stable".<P>Also, BAR checks the trans. You can't stick a manual trans on a Buick SC. It was never available in California. Many test stations puke when they look at the stifter, see a manual, got to the screen and find the manual trans selection is not in the list.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||09-15-1999||02:33 PM||||I don't know anything about California rules and regs, but I do have a V8 Fiero and can advise you on some of your other questions. I'd budget at least $2,000 assuming you're doing the work yourself and are good at fabricating and modifying. Double that amount if you're farming out the labor. It is not a real easy swap. Ogre is right about it affecting handling. I'm spending a lot of money and time on the suspension and brakes so that I can get SOME of that good Fiero handling back. The exhaust work is a bitch unless you know a custom muffler shop that is really good. As far as gas mileage, I haven't actually measured that yet, but I do know it sucks the gas down pretty bad compared to the 30-31 mpg of my V6 Fiero and the 33-35 mpg of my 4 cylinder Fiero (highway figures). I'm guessing no better than 20 mpg highway and much, much less in city driving. You can't keep your foot off the gas petal with a V8 Fiero. Gotta hear those four barrels kicking in...what a sound. V8 Fieros are play toys, not everyday drivers.||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||09-15-1999||03:40 PM||||Just give BAR a call (not CARB). Tell them what you want to do. They can give the details. You will need an engine of the same year or newer. If possible, get the VIN for the donor car.<P>Whatever you do, give them ALL of the details, be honest, get the persons name, wait two days and call again with the same question. Hammer out any differences in the answer. Get their name too.<P>Once the conversion is done, call BAR again and make an appointment with the referee. They will take about 2 hours going over the car. If there is any issues, they will tell you what to correct. If they reject the swap, immediately call one of the two people you talked to. Let them argue out the difference in requirements. When it's done, they will put a sticker in the door to tell the test stations what car to smog it to. You are done and on your way.<P>The bottom line is that BAR is dang good about swaps. They just want them done correctly. They actually get a kick out of some of the high power swaps that get done.<P>The potential gotcha's are:<BR>1. The V8 you selected needs dual cats. They will not fit in the Fiero.<BR>2. You tried to stick an automatic only motor on a manual trans. That could be a BIG problem.<BR>3. The engine is a hodge-podge of parts (e.g, 1984 block with 1988 induction). If they check the block code, they could reject it. That's where the VIN is handy.<BR>4. The engine is from a year earlier than the car. Won't fly.<BR>5. It must have a working CE/SES lamp.<BR>6. All canisters and air pumps must work (air injection to the cat might be a problem).<P>The best thing to do is go to a wrecker and by a complete swap motor. They will supply the engine, harness, and trans (if needed. Bar doesn't seem to worry about the trans other than a auto/manual mismatch), and the VIN of the donor car.<P>You can see where a V8 TPI swap (84 cease-fire V8 could be used in an 84), might be trouble since they had air injection. <P>Now the beauty of a Buick V6 or Chevy DOHC becomes obvious and those least wanted Fiero automatics become real valuable.<P>Of course a DOHC or Quad 4 came with manual trans.<P>Either way, go for it if you up to the work.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Greg Piet||09-15-1999||04:23 PM||||I dont know about the room, but it seems that if at all possible the fiero would be best with a 4.3 litre v6 twin turbo (cyclone motor).  Heck if it could beat the vette in a truck body, the fiero should do well.<BR>As I said though I have no idea if this motor would work in our little plastic cars!||||reg||1
Z||000006||88 Fiero||09-15-1999||05:46 PM||||Greg...why not???? I've a Fiero with a Buick Grand National Turbo engine in it...It is very sweet!!!! and wicked of course!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||09-15-1999||06:47 PM||||GN Fiero. That would do!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Standard||09-16-1999||03:28 AM||||88 Fiero,<BR>One of the guys I work with does some performance tuning (he's trying to start up a  shop, mainly VW's and Honda's) and he said that one thing he always wanted to do was swap a GN Turbo motor into a Fiero.. Also said he'd help me when I got my GT.. any advice on finding and installing one?||||reg||10
